  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGES:\n\n- Steady precipitation today south of about I-90.\n\n- Warming and drying trend Sunday into the first part of next\n  week.\n\n\nSatellite imagery early this morning indicates a cutoff upper\nlevel low pressure circulation moving across southern Idaho. A\ndeformation zone north and east of the low pressure center is\nbringing rain to north-central Idaho, along with west-central and\nsouthwest Montana. Rainfall is heavy at times, with rates of up to\nabout 0.30 inches per hour in the Clearwater and Bitterroot\nranges.  \n\nThe upper low will slide across southern Idaho throughout the day\ntoday, spreading a band of wrap-around stratiform rain with \nembedded convection from I-90 south into Lemhi County. \nPrecipitation will taper off late this afternoon and evening. \nStorm total rainfall will range from 0.50-1.50 inches from \nnorthern Lemhi County east to Butte, with isolated totals \nexceeding 2.0 inches.\n\nClearing skies tonight will favor valley fog and low stratus, \nparticularly in areas that received substantial rainfall. Upper-\nlevel ridging builds across the region Sunday and Monday, \nestablishing a warming and drying trend.\n\nWhile deterministic models diverge on the details mid to late \nnext week, ensemble clusters favor southwesterly flow, mild \ntemperatures, increased afternoon breezes, and a gradual return of\nisolated afternoon thunderstorm chances starting as early as \nTuesday. By Sunday (Sep 27), ensemble guidance (60-70% confidence)\npoints toward a deeper Pacific Northwest trough pushing inland, \nbringing cooler temperatures and higher shower chances.",
